data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The human","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" body is constantly surrounded by microscopic invaders—bacteria, viruses, fungi, and parasites—","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"all looking to exploit our biological resources. To survive, we rely on the immune system, a remarkably sophisticated defense network comprised","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of specialized cells, tissues, and organs. While our innate immune system provides a rapid, broad response to danger, it","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is the adaptive immune system that offers targeted, long-term protection. Vaccines are one of humanity's greatest medical achievements precisely","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" because they safely hijack this adaptive system, training our bodies to recognize and neutralize deadly pathogens before we ever encounter them in the wild.\n\nTo","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" understand how vaccines work, one must first understand how the immune system naturally responds to an infection. When a pathogen, such as the influenza","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" virus, breaches the body, it carries unique molecular signatures called antigens. These antigens act like a criminal’s mugshot.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Specialized white blood cells called macrophages engulf the invader and present these antigens to other immune cells, sounding the alarm.\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"This triggers the activation of T-cells and B-cells. Helper T-cells coordinate the immune response, while killer T-","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"cells destroy infected host cells. Simultaneously, B-cells mature into antibody factories. Antibodies are Y-shaped proteins that bind","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" specifically to the pathogen's antigens, neutralizing the invader and marking it for destruction by other immune cells. \n\nThis","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" initial battle takes time—often a week or two—during which the pathogen may cause severe illness while the immune system figures","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" out the correct \"blueprint\" for antibodies. However, once the battle is won, a crucial subset of T-cells and B-cells","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" transforms into \"memory cells.\" These cells linger in the body for years, sometimes decades. If the same pathogen tries to infect","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the body again, the memory cells recognize the antigens instantly. They mount a massive, rapid counter-offensive that neutralizes the threat","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" before symptoms can even develop. This is why people rarely get chickenpox twice; their immune system remembers.\n\nVaccines use","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" this exact biological memory mechanism, but without the danger of actual disease. A vaccine introduces a harmless component or weakened version of a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" pathogen into the body, effectively providing a \"wanted poster\" to the immune system. \n\nHistorically and scientifically, vaccines have been developed","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" using several different platforms:\n\n1. **Live-Attenuated Vaccines:** These use a weakened (attenuated) form","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of the live virus or bacterium. Because the microbe is so weak, it cannot cause disease in a healthy person, but it still","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" replicates enough to prompt a robust immune response and strong memory cell formation (e.g., the MMR vaccine).\n2. **","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"Inactivated Vaccines:** These use pathogens that have been killed or inactivated using heat, chemicals, or radiation. They cannot replicate","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" at all, but their antigens are still intact enough to trigger immunity. Because they generate a slightly weaker response, they often require booster","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" shots (e.g., the polio vaccine).\n3. **Subunit, Recombinant, or Polysaccharide Vaccines:** Instead of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

